  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Preparedness Capacity For Local Responders in Viet Nam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Viet Nam is enduring one of its most destructive typhoon and flood seasons in decades, with 13 named storms recorded in 2025 to date, including Typhoons Kajiki (#5), Bualoi (#10), Matmo (#11), and most recently Kalmaegi (#13). These storms have struck in rapid succession, causing widespread flooding, landslides, and infrastructure damage across both northern and central provinces. The Viet Nam Joint Response Plan for Multiple Typhoons and Floods (2025), launched in October 2025, highlights the urgent need for both immediate relief and longer-term recovery investments. IOM Viet Nam in coordination with the Viet Nam Disaster and Dyke Management Authority (VDDMA) are revising and validating national disaster preparedness training materials to reflect recent administrative reforms and evolving risk contexts. The proposed activities include stakeholder consultations, curriculum analysis and revision, and a national validation workshop. These are designed to strengthen local responder capacity and support the institutionalization of updated tools at the provincial level.</narrative>
  </description>
